#
# mvn: -- the Aqueduct extension vocabulary.
#
# Brick describes what equipment exists and how it is connected. It does not
# describe what a machine is worth, how many people it serves, or which physical
# conservation law relates a group of its readings. This file adds those, and
# the constraint instances that bind them to the two LBNL systems.
#
# Two things live here:
#   1. the vocabulary  -- mvn: properties and the mvn:Constraint class
#   2. the constraints -- one instance per physical relation, each naming the
#                         points that participate and the expression whose value
#                         is zero when the relation holds
#
# Per-asset values (criticality, cost, occupants, design values) are in
# building_extensions.ttl, not here.
#

@prefix mvn:     <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/mvn#> .
@prefix brick:   <https://brickschema.org/schema/Brick#> .
@prefix sdahu:   <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/sdahu#> .
@prefix chiller: <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/chiller#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. VOCABULARY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#

mvn:criticalityTier a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "criticality tier" ;
    rdfs:comment """How much it matters that this asset keeps working.
1 = failure interrupts occupant comfort or another asset immediately.
2 = failure degrades performance and is tolerable for days.
3 = failure is a nuisance.
Matches the criticality_tier column on app.assets, which constrains it to 1-3.
Used to rank advisories when several assets are degrading at once.""" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

mvn:replacementCostUSD a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "replacement cost (USD)" ;
    rdfs:comment """Installed cost to replace the asset outright. The
remaining-life layer needs it to answer the only question a building owner
actually asks: is it cheaper to repair this now or run it to failure.""" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal .

mvn:repairCostUSD a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "repair cost (USD)" ;
    rdfs:comment """Typical cost of the corrective action for a fault on this
asset -- a tube clean, a valve rebuild, a refrigerant recharge. Compared against
replacement cost and against the energy cost of running degraded.""" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal .

mvn:occupantsServed a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "occupants served" ;
    rdfs:comment """How many people lose comfort if this asset stops. Weights
criticality independently of cost: a cheap asset serving 200 people can outrank
an expensive one serving nobody.""" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

mvn:designValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "design value" ;
    rdfs:comment """The value this point is designed to hold or reach, in the SI
unit the measurement is stored in. Gives the baseline layer a fixed reference
that does not depend on observed data, so "normal" is not defined purely by
whatever the equipment happened to be doing. Every value asserted in this
project is derived from the source data and each one records how.""" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal .

mvn:Constraint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "physical constraint" ;
    rdfs:comment """A conservation relation that must hold among a group of
readings if the equipment and its sensors are both behaving. Residuals from
these constraints are the raw material of fault detection: a persistent non-zero
residual means either the physics is being violated or a sensor is lying, and
which of the two is the discrimination the rule engine has to make.""" .

mvn:constrainedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "constrained by" ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a constraint to each point that participates in it.
Traversed in both directions: given a constraint, fetch its inputs; given a
point that looks wrong, find every relation it takes part in and therefore every
other reading that could be the real culprit.""" ;
    rdfs:domain mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range brick:Point .

mvn:residualExpression a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "residual expression" ;
    rdfs:comment """Arithmetic expression, evaluated per timestamp, whose value
is zero when the constraint holds.

VARIABLE FORM: a database point_id in braces, for example {ahu-1.ma_temp} or
{chiller-1.cdw_leaving_temp}. The braces exist because point ids contain hyphens
and dots and so cannot be bare identifiers. Every variable is therefore a direct
key into app.measurements with nothing to resolve.

The alternative was to name the Brick model's own local names, which are the LBNL
source column names -- sdahu.MA_TEMP, chiller.CHL_SWCD_TEMP_1. Rejected because
four of those column names state the opposite of what the column contains, so an
expression written in them reads as though it has the sign backwards even when it
is right. The point ids were corrected to match their contents, so an expression
written in point ids can be checked by reading it. Nobody has to remember a
per-column exception.

mvn:constrainedBy still names the Brick model nodes, because that is what the
graph is for. The two are kept in step by the ingestion manifests, where each
point's `column` key is its Brick local name and its `point_id` is its database
key, and every constraint is checked against both.

OPERATORS: + - * / and parentheses only. Literals are SI, matching how
app.measurements stores values. No functions, no conditionals -- an expression
that needs either is a sign the relation belongs in code, not in the graph.

A residual is not expected to be exactly zero on healthy equipment. Sensor
calibration alone puts a bias on every one of these. The measured fault-free
mean and spread of each residual is recorded in its rdfs:comment below, and the
rule engine must compare against a condition-normalised baseline rather than
against zero.""" ;
    rdfs:domain mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. CONSTRAINT INSTANCES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#

# --- air side -------------------------------------------------------------

mvn:MixedAirBalance a mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed air temperature balance" ;
    rdfs:comment """Air leaving the mixing box is outdoor air and return air
blended together, so its temperature must sit between the two, at the point
determined by how much of each is present. The outdoor air damper position is
used as the blend fraction.

Residual unit: kelvin (a temperature difference).
Measured on fault-free data, July 2018, supply fan running, 4560 samples:
mean -0.369 K, sd 0.377 K, range -1.564 to +8.215 K. The small negative bias is
expected -- damper position is a nonlinear proxy for the outdoor air mass
fraction, so the linear blend understates outdoor air at mid-travel. The wide
positive tail is start-up transients, when the mixing box has not yet settled.

Fires on: outdoor air damper stuck, mixed air sensor drift, outdoor air sensor
drift. Distinguishing those three is exactly what the graph is for -- a damper
fault also disturbs the coil, a sensor fault does not.""" ;
    mvn:constrainedBy sdahu:MA_TEMP,
        sdahu:OA_TEMP,
        sdahu:RA_TEMP,
        sdahu:OA_DMPR ;
    mvn:residualExpression """{ahu-1.ma_temp} - ({ahu-1.oa_damper} * {ahu-1.oa_temp} + (1 - {ahu-1.oa_damper}) * {ahu-1.ra_temp})""" .

mvn:CoilEnergyBalance a mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Cooling coil energy balance" ;
    rdfs:comment """The coil cannot cool air below the temperature of the water
entering it, and how far toward that limit it gets should track how far the
chilled water valve is open. So the actual air temperature drop across the coil
is compared with the fraction of the maximum achievable drop that the valve is
commanding.

Residual unit: kelvin.
Measured on fault-free data, July 2018, supply fan running, 4560 samples:
mean +0.456 K, sd 1.165 K, range -3.891 to +5.993 K.

This is the constraint that spans both systems, and it is the reason the chilled
water loop edge exists: the water temperature term comes from the chiller plant,
not the air handler. Note which node supplies it: the entering water is
CWL_SEC_RW_TEMP, the node whose LBNL name says "return", because that column holds
the cold supply. The point id it maps to, chw-plant-1.sec_supply_temp, has been
corrected to say so, which is why the expression above reads the right way round.

Two limitations, both recorded rather than hidden. First,
coil water flow is not measured anywhere in the AHU dataset, so a true two-sided
energy balance -- heat out of the air equals heat into the water -- cannot be
written; the return water temperature is therefore a declared participant but
does not appear in the expression. Second, the two LBNL systems are independent
simulations with unrelated load profiles, coupled only through shared Chicago
weather, so the water temperature in this expression is not physically the water
that cooled this air. The residual is therefore approximate as a number. The
graph edge it depends on is not approximate: it is what lets a coil symptom be
traced to a chiller cause.

Fires on: cooling valve leaking, cooling valve stuck, supply air sensor drift,
and -- through the chiller plant -- loss of chilled water temperature.""" ;
    mvn:constrainedBy sdahu:SA_TEMP,
        sdahu:MA_TEMP,
        sdahu:CHWC_VLV,
        chiller:CWL_SEC_RW_TEMP,
        chiller:CWL_SEC_SW_TEMP ;
    mvn:residualExpression """({ahu-1.ma_temp} - {ahu-1.sa_temp}) - {ahu-1.chw_valve} * ({ahu-1.ma_temp} - {chw-plant-1.sec_supply_temp})""" .

# --- water side -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# One instance per chiller. The three machines are identical, have their own
# sensors, and stage independently, so a single instance bound to chiller 1
# would leave chillers 2 and 3 with no energy balance at all.
#
# All three expressions have the same shape:
#
#   heat rejected at the condenser
#     - heat absorbed at the evaporator
#     - electrical power drawn
#
# which is conservation of energy across the machine: everything the compressor
# does and everything the chilled water gives up has to leave through the
# condenser. Residual unit: watts.
#
# The constant 4.17e6 is the volumetric heat capacity of water in J/(m3 K) --
# 997 kg/m3 times 4184 J/(kg K) -- because both flows are stored as m3/s.
#
# Sign note. The condenser term is leaving minus entering, warm minus cool, which
# is what makes it a heat rejection rather than its negative. That is readable
# directly from the expression only because the point ids were renamed: the source
# columns are CHL_SWCD_TEMP for the warm side and CHL_RWCD_TEMP for the cool side,
# which is the opposite sense to the plant-level pair CDWL_SW_TEMP / CDWL_RW_TEMP
# describing the same loop. One word, two meanings, in one model. The ids now say
# entering and leaving, which Brick defines and which cannot be read two ways, so
# nobody has to hold the exception in their head while checking the arithmetic.
# Verified against the data: leaving averages 29.84 C, entering 27.44 C.
#
# Measured on fault-free data, July 2018, chiller 1, running only, 5403 samples:
# mean -99.0 kW, sd 66.3 kW, range -517.5 to +271.0 kW. The balance does not
# close. Backing compressor work out of the two thermal terms implies about
# 112 kW where the power channel reports 355 kW, a coefficient of performance of
# 6.1 against the 1.9 the reported power gives -- 6.1 is the plausible figure for
# a water-cooled chiller. So the reported power and the thermal terms disagree in
# the source simulation. This residual is usable as a trend, because fouling and
# refrigerant loss both move it, but its absolute value is not a physical energy
# imbalance and must never be thresholded at zero.

mvn:ChillerEnergyBalance_1 a mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Chiller 1 energy balance" ;
    mvn:constrainedBy chiller:CHL_CW_FLOW_1,
        chiller:CHL_RW_TEMP_1,
        chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_1,
        chiller:CHL_CD_FLOW_1,
        chiller:CHL_SWCD_TEMP_1,
        chiller:CHL_RWCD_TEMP_1,
        chiller:CHL_POW_1 ;
    mvn:residualExpression """({chiller-1.cdw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-1.cdw_leaving_temp} - {chiller-1.cdw_entering_temp})) - ({chiller-1.chw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-1.chw_return_temp} - {chiller-1.chw_supply_temp})) - {chiller-1.power}""" .

mvn:ChillerEnergyBalance_2 a mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Chiller 2 energy balance" ;
    mvn:constrainedBy chiller:CHL_CW_FLOW_2,
        chiller:CHL_RW_TEMP_2,
        chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_2,
        chiller:CHL_CD_FLOW_2,
        chiller:CHL_SWCD_TEMP_2,
        chiller:CHL_RWCD_TEMP_2,
        chiller:CHL_POW_2 ;
    mvn:residualExpression """({chiller-2.cdw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-2.cdw_leaving_temp} - {chiller-2.cdw_entering_temp})) - ({chiller-2.chw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-2.chw_return_temp} - {chiller-2.chw_supply_temp})) - {chiller-2.power}""" .

mvn:ChillerEnergyBalance_3 a mvn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Chiller 3 energy balance" ;
    mvn:constrainedBy chiller:CHL_CW_FLOW_3,
        chiller:CHL_RW_TEMP_3,
        chiller:CHL_SW_TEMP_3,
        chiller:CHL_CD_FLOW_3,
        chiller:CHL_SWCD_TEMP_3,
        chiller:CHL_RWCD_TEMP_3,
        chiller:CHL_POW_3 ;
    mvn:residualExpression """({chiller-3.cdw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-3.cdw_leaving_temp} - {chiller-3.cdw_entering_temp})) - ({chiller-3.chw_flow} * 4.17e6 * ({chiller-3.chw_return_temp} - {chiller-3.chw_supply_temp})) - {chiller-3.power}""" .
